This complete, zero-third-party-library architecture and implementation enables NSSF staff to validate, encrypt, and dispatch member statements via Email (SMTP) and WhatsApp, packaged as a single embedded binary.

### Architectural & Zero-Dependency Notes

* **PDF Parsing & Encryption (Zero Third-Party Go Libraries):** Since external Go packages (like `pdfcpu` or `unipdf`) are prohibited, the backend uses Go's standard library (`compress/zlib`, `regexp`, `bytes`, `crypto/rc4`, `crypto/md5`) to decompress PDF `FlateDecode` streams, extract member metadata (`NSSF Member No`, `Id Card No`, `Member Name`), and inject standard PDF 1.4 RC4-128 encryption dictionaries (`V=2`, `R=3`) directly into the raw PDF xref table using the member's National ID as the user password.
* **SQLite Without CGo/Third-Party Drivers:** To maintain pure Go compilation with zero external dependencies, you can either invoke the system `sqlite3` CLI via `os/exec` with `-json` output flags or implement an atomic JSON-backed WAL store. Below uses `os/exec` wrapping `sqlite3` (pre-installed on Linux/macOS and available as a single executable on Windows) so real SQLite tables, queries, and ACID transactions work with zero Go module dependencies.
* **WhatsApp Integration:** Because WhatsApp Web's Noise Protocol + Protobuf + Curve25519 state machine cannot be cleanly maintained without `whatsmeow`, the zero-dependency approach bridges either to an official Meta WhatsApp Cloud API endpoint (via standard `net/http`) or a local WhatsApp Web bridge sidecar over localhost HTTP, rendering the QR code SVG/base64 directly in the Settings UI.

